hello and welcome to maths amplified in  this video we are going to learn about  the simple interrupted sutures  interrupted sutures are the most  commonly used technique in the closure  of skin it is known as interrupted  because the individual stitches aren't  connected they are separate and if one  suture fails the others hold the wound  in place it is a simple technique and  relatively easy to place by beginners  this is the third video in the suturing  technique for beginners video series  brought to you by med simplified in  collaboration with kids of medicine we  will be using this suturing practice  gate to learn about these techniques  which was sent to me by kids of medicine  this skit is excellent for beginners and  contains all the things that you need to  begin learning about suturing check the  link below in the description and use  the coupon code med 10 to get a 10%  discount on this kit okay so let's begin  with the technique of simple interrupted  sutures to begin the first step is to  load the suture material and your needle  in the needle holder you hold the needle  at the junction of the distal 2/3 and  the proximal 1/3 of the needle this  imparts the ability to the needle and  your movements after loading the needle  lock the needle holder so that your  needle remains fixed and stable to start  suturing the first step is to gently  lift the skin with the forceps this  allows you to visualize the wound and  the skin properly and plan your entry  and exit points exactly pierce the skin  surface with a needle at a 90 degree  angle to the skin the needle should not  be tilted as it may lead to a more  superficial or deeper entry into the  wound  keeping the needle at a perpendicular  angle allows your movements to be  precise and accurate  the entry point should be four  millimeters from the wound edge the next  step is to pass the needle through one  side of the skin of the wound and make  it rise out of the middle of the wound  to do this you have to use a curved  motion of your wrist that allows the  curved needle to easily pass through the  skin as the needle rises out in the  middle of the wound use your forceps to  hold your needle as you release your  needle holder grab the needle again with  your needle holder the next part  consists of passing the needle in a  similar style of motion through the  opposite edge of the wound  to do this we again lift the opposing  skin edge gently with the forceps to  visualize the wound properly we pierce  the skin in the middle of the wound and  using the same curved motion of our  wrist the needle travels perpendicularly  through the dermis from inside to  outside  we use the forceps to grasp the needle  and stabilize it until we hold the  needle again without needle holder  using your needle holder pull the suture  material through the wound leaving a  three to four centimeter tail behind for  the final part of tying the knot  to tie the knot you grasp the needle  holder in one hand and the suture in  your other hand the first step is to  make two loops of the suture material  around your needle holder then grasp the  tail end of the suture material with  your needle holder and pass it in  between those two loops of suture that  you just made this is your first knot  move you're not at this side of the  wound so that it doesn't lay directly  over the wound after this make one more  loop of the suture thread around the  needle holder again grasp the tail end  of the suture and pull it through the  loop that you just made this second knot  imparts stability to the sutures and  ensures that the knot won't open up  spontaneously now depending upon the  conditions of the wound and how much  strength you want in your sutures you  can place one to two more of these  single loop knots over your first knot  pull both ends of the suture with your  needle holder and trim the suture to one  to two centimeters length it is  important not to trim the sutures to a  very short length as it makes removing  those sutures very difficult after a few  days  you can also check out the met  simplified suturing PDF guide which  helps you to learn suturing in a better  way a clear and concise guide that  includes step-by-step learning of  various techniques in a simple and easy  to understand presentation keep it in  your phone and follow along as you  practice you can get this copy by  joining meThe simplifies community on  patreon.com plus you will also unlock  many other cool features like flashcards  from all other videos members exclusive  content and much more thank you so much  for watching guys see you in the next  video
 

